1
00:00:23,716 --> 00:00:29,961
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought we can discuss the gradual training that is found in suttas.

2
00:00:34,144 --> 00:00:36,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sometimes divided in different ways, you know.

3
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:42,130
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is the usual.

4
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:47,834
[SPEAKER_01]: In Majjhima Nikaya, 107, it begins with

5
00:00:49,335 --> 00:00:59,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it possible to describe a gradual training, a gradual practice and gradual progress in the dhamma and discipline?

6
00:01:01,508 --> 00:01:02,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says, yes.

7
00:01:03,910 --> 00:01:09,055
[SPEAKER_01]: When he obtains a person to be tamed, he first disciplines him thus.

8
00:01:10,196 --> 00:01:12,339
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, come Bhikkhu, be virtuous.

9
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:18,364
[SPEAKER_01]: In some other suttas, it's also before that, you know, five precepts, eight precepts.

10
00:01:18,384 --> 00:01:22,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So it is, overall, that's the first step.

11
00:01:22,107 --> 00:01:22,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue.

12
00:01:22,507 --> 00:01:24,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking on the precepts.

13
00:01:39,626 --> 00:02:03,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think the seeing fear in the slightest fault, being able to recognize a fault and then... Yeah, well, it's when it says restrained by the rules of patimokkha, like the rules that pertain to practice, not kind of the rules they were pertaining to like a social circumstances at the time, they all can be boiled down into eight precepts.

14
00:02:05,931 --> 00:02:12,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Or ten, if you want to forsake the handling of money and gold and so on.

15
00:02:13,555 --> 00:02:17,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it's not just for the monks, it's for anybody who wants to practice.

16
00:02:17,797 --> 00:02:22,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it was presumed that by wearing robes you want to practice.

17
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:24,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what the Buddha would be addressing the monks.

18
00:02:24,981 --> 00:02:31,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you don't wear the robes today, you want to practice, rules of patimokkha apply to you.

19
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:33,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Not technically,

20
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:51,002
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as in the spirit of eight precepts, yes, that's why whenever the Buddha would speak in the context outside of the like a monk saṅgha, speak about the virtue, he would talk about, you know, the like the eight precepts, the wise men of the old used to, you know, used to live by eight precepts all the time.

21
00:02:51,042 --> 00:02:53,564
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they had such a long lifespan and so on.

22
00:02:55,044 --> 00:03:02,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eight precepts is the bare minimum for beginning of that self-taming, self-sense-restraint.

23
00:03:03,327 --> 00:03:03,927
[SPEAKER_00]: The discipline.

24
00:03:03,987 --> 00:03:05,068
[SPEAKER_00]: The discipline, exactly.

25
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:09,650
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, if you're a disciple, then there's the discipline.

26
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:11,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yes, yes, exactly.

27
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:18,693
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are a lay follower, which means the one who trails behind out of devotion, that's where the five precepts are.

28
00:03:19,593 --> 00:03:25,774
[SPEAKER_00]: They keep you from behaving like an animal, and you're making merit, and hopefully it will contribute towards your better birth.

29
00:03:26,234 --> 00:03:34,996
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're the one who sees himself as as the one who practices the dhamma, the eight precepts are not negotiable.

30
00:03:35,036 --> 00:03:37,576
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the the bare minimum, the basis.

31
00:03:38,516 --> 00:03:39,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the very first thing.

32
00:03:40,577 --> 00:03:42,377
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can substitute there.

33
00:03:42,457 --> 00:03:43,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Come here, Bhikkhu.

34
00:03:43,217 --> 00:03:44,757
[SPEAKER_00]: You say, you want to practice?

35
00:03:44,837 --> 00:03:45,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, come here.

36
00:03:46,198 --> 00:03:47,898
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the discipline you have to take on.

37
00:03:49,399 --> 00:03:53,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you take on that discipline, you need to start seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

38
00:03:54,241 --> 00:04:01,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that can be taken neurotically and probably would... will be initially, like... but that's... that's fine.

39
00:04:01,864 --> 00:04:11,928
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you... you... you get used to the neurotic aspect of it, so you stop being bothered by neurosis, but you still remain seeing the... the value of that discipline and the fear of breaking it.

40
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:20,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, if you're neurotic about breaking the precepts, that neurosis, that fear, will actually protect your discipline.

41
00:04:21,455 --> 00:04:24,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like heriotica, fear and shame of wrongdoing.

42
00:04:25,719 --> 00:04:31,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're afraid because of all the, you know, well, it will undo your very striving.

43
00:04:32,524 --> 00:04:42,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that you want to develop through your practice can only be developed on the basis of discipline, and here you are willingly eroding that discipline through your choices.

44
00:04:43,483 --> 00:04:44,303
[SPEAKER_00]: To break the rules.

45
00:04:44,384 --> 00:04:48,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start contemplating on the dangers of doing that.

46
00:04:48,546 --> 00:04:50,467
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, naturally, you feel afraid of doing it.

47
00:04:51,908 --> 00:04:52,869
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you won't do it.

48
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:54,550
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you won't be careless about it.

49
00:04:55,210 --> 00:04:58,012
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not afraid, that's when people become careless.

50
00:04:58,092 --> 00:05:00,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stop paying close attention.

51
00:05:00,653 --> 00:05:04,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no fear motivating you.

52
00:05:04,055 --> 00:05:05,096
[SPEAKER_00]: I kind of just jumped to the...

53
00:05:07,398 --> 00:05:09,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting rid of the jhāna part.

54
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,402
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go to the jhāna part first.

55
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:13,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I work my way the other way around.

56
00:05:15,587 --> 00:05:19,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure you can find an article or a teacher that will say, yes, you can do it.

57
00:05:20,051 --> 00:05:23,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You can bypass everything the Buddha said and go straight to jhāna part.

58
00:05:23,435 --> 00:05:26,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the jhāna, the results of that jhāna...

59
00:05:28,811 --> 00:05:34,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure, sure.

60
00:05:36,797 --> 00:05:42,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the common kind of misconception of even people that keep the precepts,

61
00:05:43,609 --> 00:05:47,191
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't necessarily see the direct value of it.

62
00:05:48,231 --> 00:05:53,833
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see how is this helping me tame myself, in that sense.

63
00:05:54,594 --> 00:05:57,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, to what extent am I developing through keeping the precepts.

64
00:05:58,015 --> 00:06:03,858
[SPEAKER_00]: They do it on account of, you know, faith, culture, tradition, whatever.

65
00:06:04,718 --> 00:06:28,444
[SPEAKER_00]: meditation group they're part of, they might do it on account of external circumstances, motivating them to be restrained and keep the precepts because it's a good thing and so on, universally speaking, but deep down, they still hold the view that all these random things that they do will then, somehow, magically, contribute to the special experience of a jhāna or enlightenment.

66
00:06:29,364 --> 00:06:32,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that happens, the work's done.

67
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:45,831
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they don't see, basically, in other words, a direct work that they can do, through their own choices and developing the right context, that will inevitably have to result in jhāna and enlightenment.

68
00:06:46,654 --> 00:06:58,783
[SPEAKER_00]: They just do random work, that then they hope will produce the experience of jhāna or enlightenment, and then they won't have to do any work, because they're enlightened.

69
00:07:01,105 --> 00:07:03,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, let's go into the second step.

70
00:07:03,727 --> 00:07:08,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, once that person has perfected virtue,

71
00:07:11,253 --> 00:07:12,975
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha disciplines him further.

72
00:07:14,156 --> 00:07:17,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Kambeku, guard the doors of your sense faculties.

73
00:07:17,699 --> 00:07:21,543
[SPEAKER_01]: On seeing a form with the eye, do not grasp at its signs and features.

74
00:07:22,384 --> 00:07:31,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Since if you were to leave the eye faculty, and so on, unguarded, evil, unwholesome states of covetousness and grief might invade you.

75
00:07:31,272 --> 00:07:32,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice the way of its restraint.

76
00:07:33,314 --> 00:07:33,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

77
00:07:34,143 --> 00:07:39,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already the, you know, what we call, colloquially, in our talks, like the peripheral awareness.

78
00:07:40,508 --> 00:07:43,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't grasp the signs and features of that which you've seen.

79
00:07:44,731 --> 00:07:49,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like you don't see anything.

80
00:07:49,075 --> 00:07:56,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the signs and features of that which you... of a sense object you experience through your senses, they're always peripheral.

81
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:58,282
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see signs and features.

82
00:07:58,882 --> 00:08:01,004
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like when you look at the signs that says, stop.

83
00:08:02,257 --> 00:08:12,687
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the letters, you see the color, you see the shape, but the significance of do not pass this line, you, specifically, don't come here, stop there, that's not in the sign.

84
00:08:13,208 --> 00:08:16,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you see that when your eyes see the sign.

85
00:08:16,591 --> 00:08:19,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the features of that sign, the significance of it.

86
00:08:19,474 --> 00:08:20,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, the meaning, the significance.

87
00:08:20,655 --> 00:08:24,999
[SPEAKER_00]: The meaning, sure, the meaning, the significance, the signs, the features, the nimittas, basically.

88
00:08:25,780 --> 00:08:34,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason I'm saying that is because often that practice of sense restraint is kind of conflated with this view of beating the sense down.

89
00:08:34,969 --> 00:08:38,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, not looking, not looking, not touching, not breathing, nothing, nothing.

90
00:08:38,952 --> 00:08:41,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Just compress it all, not see anything.

91
00:08:42,295 --> 00:08:44,157
[SPEAKER_00]: But still you're not seeing the significance of that.

92
00:08:44,437 --> 00:08:46,840
[SPEAKER_00]: The signs and features of what you're doing, you're not seeing.

93
00:08:47,536 --> 00:08:51,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where you need to start basically recognizing the sense restraint to be.

94
00:08:52,718 --> 00:09:06,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Are your restraining senses sure beforehand, as we said before, before you do actions, you decide, you already know whether it's going to be rooted in aversion, greed, and so on, and that if you know then it's rooted in greed, you don't do it.

95
00:09:07,524 --> 00:09:10,125
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, say, sometimes things surprise you, pop up,

96
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:16,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure arises that can become an ill will or sensuality.

97
00:09:17,435 --> 00:09:20,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you have a choice of that which you have seen.

98
00:09:21,258 --> 00:09:32,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a choice whether you're choosing to dwell mentally on the signs and features that are suitable for increasing of passion in regard to it, or the signs and features of the...

99
00:09:32,448 --> 00:09:32,988
[SPEAKER_00]: They are also...

100
00:09:33,189 --> 00:09:33,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Both are there.

101
00:09:34,169 --> 00:09:35,471
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just what you choose to attend.

102
00:09:36,291 --> 00:09:38,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the signs and features that are not suitable.

103
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:40,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, pressure has arisen.

104
00:09:41,157 --> 00:09:42,181
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't undo that.

105
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:43,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You've seen things.

106
00:09:44,233 --> 00:09:48,835
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, okay, like, I'm not in control of these eyes, only relatively speaking.

107
00:09:48,915 --> 00:09:57,058
[SPEAKER_00]: I can sort of foresee as much as I can and prevent, you know, things that are suitable for lust or aversion to arise for me to see and so on.

108
00:09:57,118 --> 00:10:01,139
[SPEAKER_00]: But, inevitably, things will happen because the organs are still there, untamed.

109
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:07,602
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that does happen, that's when we say you need to be able to allow things to endure without breaking your discipline.

110
00:10:08,082 --> 00:10:08,722
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the order.

111
00:10:08,762 --> 00:10:10,163
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you have the discipline first.

112
00:10:11,081 --> 00:10:14,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Without, like, flirting with breaking of the rules, because you see the danger of it.

113
00:10:15,243 --> 00:10:19,386
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, without losing the context of the signs and features of that which pressures you.

114
00:10:20,907 --> 00:10:31,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's on that level of signs and features, of things that pressure you, that mind either delights in sensuality, or tries to reject and get rid of the discomfort.

115
00:10:33,234 --> 00:10:34,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the root of the whole problem.

116
00:10:35,614 --> 00:10:40,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to be restraining your senses correctly, it's... it's kind of... well, it's effortless.

117
00:10:40,797 --> 00:10:50,942
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to be beating your eye down, you know, cutting your ears off and so on, so you'd never experience the object that might cause pressure of lust or disagreeability to arise in you.

118
00:10:53,343 --> 00:10:57,105
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is make sure, when your eye does see, is do here,

119
00:10:58,044 --> 00:11:03,786
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't dwell, delight, accept, welcome, entertain.

120
00:11:04,386 --> 00:11:09,287
[SPEAKER_00]: The signs and features of what you've seen, they're basically making lust to increase.

121
00:11:11,007 --> 00:11:20,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sign of the beauty, the sign of agreeability, the sign of non-danger, the sign of friendly, homely, non-threatening.

122
00:11:21,070 --> 00:11:24,031
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things, all these significances are the sign of sensuality.

123
00:11:24,831 --> 00:11:27,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if people were to truly see the danger of sensuality,

124
00:11:27,912 --> 00:11:33,157
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that would ever be seen ever again as friendly, suitable, acceptable, pleasurable for me.

125
00:11:33,317 --> 00:11:36,480
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be seen like an ember, like a pit of burning embers.

126
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:42,466
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can also see it with both those significances, what, you know, what you used to see it as.

127
00:11:42,726 --> 00:11:43,247
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

128
00:11:43,267 --> 00:11:44,408
[SPEAKER_00]: The significances are there.

129
00:11:44,548 --> 00:11:47,831
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like I must remove these significances of pleasure.

130
00:11:47,871 --> 00:11:48,272
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

131
00:11:48,612 --> 00:11:51,154
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop entertaining them

132
00:11:51,955 --> 00:11:58,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And look for the other significances that are equally there, that I've been ignoring out of my habits of welcoming lust.

133
00:11:59,481 --> 00:12:05,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can look at the beautiful parts, which increases lust, or I can look at the ugly parts, which will calm things down.

134
00:12:06,106 --> 00:12:11,771
[SPEAKER_00]: But not for the sake of getting rid of the beautiful parts, or for the sake of getting rid of the pressure.

135
00:12:12,251 --> 00:12:13,172
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not on you.

136
00:12:13,692 --> 00:12:17,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Your eye is pressured, and your eye wants the parts that are beautiful.

137
00:12:17,515 --> 00:12:20,758
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is just keep projecting,

138
00:12:23,599 --> 00:12:30,922
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha would say.

139
00:12:30,942 --> 00:12:34,083
[SPEAKER_00]: In beautiful, if you develop your mind, you'll be able to see the ugly.

140
00:12:34,103 --> 00:12:34,744
[SPEAKER_00]: In the beautiful.

141
00:12:35,144 --> 00:12:37,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Not change the beautiful into ugly.

142
00:12:38,005 --> 00:12:39,286
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same beautiful.

143
00:12:40,066 --> 00:12:41,667
[SPEAKER_00]: The same beautiful significances.

144
00:12:43,047 --> 00:12:44,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see them as ugly.

145
00:12:45,670 --> 00:12:47,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Through them, you see the ugly.

146
00:12:47,571 --> 00:12:48,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the threatening.

147
00:12:48,651 --> 00:12:49,472
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the danger.

148
00:12:49,912 --> 00:12:54,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they do not grasp at its significance.

149
00:12:54,274 --> 00:12:54,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

150
00:12:54,554 --> 00:12:56,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take it up.

151
00:12:56,335 --> 00:12:56,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it.

152
00:12:57,015 --> 00:12:57,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't entertain it.

153
00:12:57,795 --> 00:13:01,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take it up.

154
00:13:01,957 --> 00:13:02,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's there.

155
00:13:03,758 --> 00:13:04,658
[SPEAKER_00]: The significance is there.

156
00:13:05,018 --> 00:13:05,719
[SPEAKER_00]: You take it up.

157
00:13:06,879 --> 00:13:11,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't take it up if you start attending the significances that you don't want to take up.

158
00:13:12,433 --> 00:13:15,714
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance of danger, dissatisfaction...

159
00:13:15,734 --> 00:13:16,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you should grasp at those.

160
00:13:18,734 --> 00:13:20,295
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance of danger, yeah, sure.

161
00:13:21,415 --> 00:13:25,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, in a way, you don't grasp at its signs and features.

162
00:13:26,856 --> 00:13:29,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the thing that caused you lustful.

163
00:13:29,157 --> 00:13:32,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, since if you were to leave the high faculty unguarded... Yeah.

164
00:13:32,618 --> 00:13:35,598
[SPEAKER_01]: ...evil, unwholesome states of covetousness might invade you.

165
00:13:35,798 --> 00:13:41,920
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you... so, you're saying... the other signs and features...

166
00:13:42,862 --> 00:13:44,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like, you don't start neutrally.

167
00:13:45,344 --> 00:13:48,887
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't begin your practice where you have this equal choice of beautiful and ugly.

168
00:13:50,408 --> 00:13:56,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You begin by being fully gripped, pressured, taken up, welcome, entertained, of the beautiful.

169
00:13:58,174 --> 00:14:00,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your sensory strain begins by not doing that.

170
00:14:01,417 --> 00:14:04,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't grasp those features and then the other features will become more apparent.

171
00:14:06,429 --> 00:14:15,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's... you then, by all means, grasp the feature of ugly, stick to it, and it will, like, develop dispassion into the arahantship.

172
00:14:15,514 --> 00:14:16,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncertainty, yeah.

173
00:14:17,395 --> 00:14:22,478
[SPEAKER_00]: But those features are not visible because of the automatic grasp of lust.

174
00:14:22,538 --> 00:14:27,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatic grasp of getting rid of the discomfort, when you're touched by painful.

175
00:14:27,741 --> 00:14:30,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatic grasp of distraction when you're touched by neutral feeling.

176
00:14:33,346 --> 00:14:40,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, grasping at its signs and features means, in the beginning, means unguarded, you know, you're completely unguarded.

177
00:14:40,792 --> 00:14:41,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there is no more guarding there.

178
00:14:42,113 --> 00:14:43,394
[SPEAKER_00]: You're straight into it.

179
00:14:43,434 --> 00:14:54,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you guard your senses by maintaining the context that will basically make you abstain from just habitually grasping onto the beautiful aspects of what's given to you.

180
00:14:58,826 --> 00:15:00,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And that pertains to any given sense.

181
00:15:04,138 --> 00:15:05,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that sense, you won't be.

182
00:15:05,279 --> 00:15:05,659
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

183
00:15:05,679 --> 00:15:16,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you will never be able to welcome and delight, with lust, the significance of danger in sensuality.

184
00:15:16,926 --> 00:15:18,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That needs to be developed.

185
00:15:19,387 --> 00:15:22,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you cannot have lust towards being free from sensuality.

186
00:15:23,029 --> 00:15:23,670
[SPEAKER_00]: That's inconceivable.

187
00:15:23,690 --> 00:15:24,871
[SPEAKER_00]: That's contradiction in terms.

188
00:15:25,771 --> 00:15:31,955
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will develop those signs and features by not grasping that which is suitable for grasping.

189
00:15:33,292 --> 00:15:38,859
[SPEAKER_00]: sensuality, signs of beautiful, welcoming it, entertaining it, giving in and trying to get rid of it.

190
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:41,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like the underlying tendency.

191
00:15:41,582 --> 00:15:42,803
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't underlie everything.

192
00:15:42,823 --> 00:15:44,045
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot underlie everything.

193
00:15:44,105 --> 00:15:49,091
[SPEAKER_00]: If underlying tendency to a lust can underlie everything, you won't be able to become an arahant.

194
00:15:50,687 --> 00:15:55,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you only need to abandon things that are underlined by... underlied by lust, aversion, delusion.

195
00:15:55,888 --> 00:15:56,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't grasp to them.

196
00:15:57,429 --> 00:16:01,890
[SPEAKER_00]: The signs and features that are then revealed through not grasping, you can't grasp.

197
00:16:01,910 --> 00:16:04,611
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you can't delight in lust after jhāna.

198
00:16:08,092 --> 00:16:09,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how you enter jhāna.

199
00:16:10,372 --> 00:16:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: By fully withdrawing yourself from signs and features that are grasped with lust, for lust, for increasing of that lust.

200
00:16:17,074 --> 00:16:19,395
[SPEAKER_01]: There are only signs and features that can be grasped.

201
00:16:20,289 --> 00:16:21,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's where the grasping is.

202
00:16:21,849 --> 00:16:22,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

203
00:16:22,590 --> 00:16:24,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Lustful aversion and delusion, yeah.

204
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:30,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't grasp that, and then, by all means, try and grasp the signs of ugliness.

205
00:16:31,292 --> 00:16:31,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

206
00:16:31,892 --> 00:16:34,412
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just been mental cultivation of ugliness.

207
00:16:35,193 --> 00:16:40,214
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't grasp it with lust, but you can do it with effort, and, and, and discernment.

208
00:16:40,354 --> 00:16:42,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, this is worth of keeping in my mind.

209
00:16:42,834 --> 00:16:49,096
[SPEAKER_00]: These signs and features, these nimittas, are worth to be developed and adhere to, because they result in dispassion everywhere else.

210
00:16:50,370 --> 00:17:00,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the third step, so to speak, the bhikkhu guards the doors of his sense faculties, then the tathāgata disciplines him further.

211
00:17:01,633 --> 00:17:03,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Kam bhikkhu, be moderate in eating.

212
00:17:04,634 --> 00:17:21,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Reflecting wisely, you should take food neither for amusements nor for intoxication, nor for the sake of physical beauty and attractiveness, but only for endurance and continuance of this body, for ending discomfort and for assisting the holy life.

213
00:17:22,703 --> 00:17:27,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Considering thus, I shall terminate old feelings without arousing new feelings.

214
00:17:27,466 --> 00:17:30,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I shall be healthy and blameless and shall live in comfort.

215
00:17:30,977 --> 00:17:39,303
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered the food thing in many other talks, but yeah, you just need to keep the... purify and sustain the right motivation, the reasons for you... you have to eat.

216
00:17:40,184 --> 00:17:40,944
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional.

217
00:17:41,285 --> 00:17:42,245
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point of that.

218
00:17:42,285 --> 00:17:44,907
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you need to be moderate and reflect on it.

219
00:17:45,748 --> 00:17:52,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... don't think that being moderate as in I'll eat half of this constitutes being moderate in eating.

220
00:17:53,633 --> 00:17:54,974
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll eat half of what I want to eat.

221
00:17:55,074 --> 00:17:55,234
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

222
00:17:55,595 --> 00:17:58,617
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically asceticism and more often than not it's misguided.

223
00:17:59,902 --> 00:18:04,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You're moderating eating by not losing the context of the right reasons for your eating.

224
00:18:04,684 --> 00:18:06,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Sutta describes.

225
00:18:07,486 --> 00:18:08,706
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you moderating eating?

226
00:18:09,226 --> 00:18:12,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by eat every third morsel that you've taken.

227
00:18:12,848 --> 00:18:13,008
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

228
00:18:13,108 --> 00:18:24,314
[SPEAKER_00]: You're moderating eating by not eating for the reasons of beautification, by not eating for the reasons of enjoyment, by not eating for the reasons of distraction and sensual attraction.

229
00:18:25,215 --> 00:18:29,859
[SPEAKER_00]: But by eating for the reasons of sustaining the body, getting rid of discomfort, so that I can practice.

230
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:34,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And that motivation is something you can choose to keep in your mind or ignore.

231
00:18:36,085 --> 00:18:41,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you keep that motivation in the mind, you won't have to worry about overeating or undereating, doesn't matter.

232
00:18:42,971 --> 00:18:48,817
[SPEAKER_00]: You eat for the sake of getting rid of discomfort and you eat, you know, for the sake of being alive.

233
00:18:53,423 --> 00:18:59,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often moderation in eating gets conflated with, you know, I eat 20% and I reach...

234
00:18:59,685 --> 00:19:00,605
[SPEAKER_00]: I fulfill that thing.

235
00:19:00,766 --> 00:19:01,266
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you haven't.

236
00:19:01,366 --> 00:19:04,627
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still equally ignorant of your motivation behind eating.

237
00:19:04,987 --> 00:19:07,608
[SPEAKER_00]: You still... do you see that you are not eating?

238
00:19:07,628 --> 00:19:11,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the enjoyment... eating for the sake of enjoyment is still there, but you're just reducing it to 20%.

239
00:19:12,189 --> 00:19:13,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same centrality.

240
00:19:14,090 --> 00:19:16,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if it's 100% or 20%, the centrality is there.

241
00:19:18,701 --> 00:19:26,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you really clear about the context behind your eating, on account of which you're eating, and where that context is rooted in?

242
00:19:26,867 --> 00:19:28,628
[SPEAKER_00]: What are your intentions for taking this food?

243
00:19:29,809 --> 00:19:31,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it just because you really crave the taste?

244
00:19:33,592 --> 00:19:37,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... it's... it's... like... nutritious for you?

245
00:19:37,234 --> 00:19:38,475
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that for a fact?

246
00:19:38,535 --> 00:19:38,895
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

247
00:19:39,276 --> 00:19:39,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you know that?

248
00:19:40,457 --> 00:19:40,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see that?

249
00:19:41,037 --> 00:19:42,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see why you are eating when you're eating?

250
00:19:43,759 --> 00:19:45,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel hunger when you're eating?

251
00:19:48,321 --> 00:19:49,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you worry about?

252
00:19:49,522 --> 00:19:50,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I have to have no...

253
00:19:50,503 --> 00:19:51,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to have that.

254
00:19:51,463 --> 00:19:53,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but if somebody takes that, I will not have it again.

255
00:19:54,245 --> 00:19:55,486
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that already it's obvious.

256
00:19:55,887 --> 00:19:56,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, there you go.

257
00:19:56,507 --> 00:19:57,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not eating for the right reasons.

258
00:20:01,130 --> 00:20:11,118
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is any form of passion in regard to food, you're not eating for the right reasons, which means you need to be dwelling, contemplating and discerning those reasons more clearly, further.

259
00:20:11,658 --> 00:20:13,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, okay, I will not eat ever again.

260
00:20:14,578 --> 00:20:18,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is like, you know, I can't untie this knot, so I'll just cut it.

261
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:22,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That's usually people's take on this.

262
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:24,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, just don't eat it.

263
00:20:26,761 --> 00:20:28,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't eat it if you don't need to eat it.

264
00:20:30,662 --> 00:20:33,062
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think you'll be fine without eating it, fine, don't eat it then.

265
00:20:34,243 --> 00:20:37,603
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't eat it because I can't figure out where my motivation is rooted.

266
00:20:38,084 --> 00:20:40,124
[SPEAKER_00]: That's throwing away the baby with the bathwater.

267
00:20:41,724 --> 00:20:43,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is what the practice is.

268
00:20:44,338 --> 00:20:45,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Re-rooting your motivation correctly.

269
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:48,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Re-rooting?

270
00:20:48,701 --> 00:20:48,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

271
00:20:49,221 --> 00:20:54,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's, again, you begin by eating out of sensuality, beautification, craving to get rid of the discomfort, and so on.

272
00:20:54,824 --> 00:20:56,505
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not eating with a clear mind.

273
00:20:57,466 --> 00:20:59,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm eating because there is hunger.

274
00:20:59,227 --> 00:21:00,448
[SPEAKER_00]: When I finish, I stop eating.

275
00:21:01,008 --> 00:21:03,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And particularly, like, from that point of view, eating is quite a chore.

276
00:21:03,930 --> 00:21:04,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you eat.

277
00:21:05,671 --> 00:21:12,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Having to eat, having to feel heavy afterwards for a bit, you know, having that food, that's all quite a nuisance, all the time.

278
00:21:14,407 --> 00:21:16,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see it for what it is.

279
00:21:16,288 --> 00:21:17,388
[SPEAKER_00]: A necessity, a chore.

280
00:21:19,729 --> 00:21:26,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But as you do that, you're gonna have those moments, you want to eat more, you want to... Well, exactly, yeah.

281
00:21:27,012 --> 00:21:30,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you have those moments, if you sustain the context,

282
00:21:31,997 --> 00:21:34,799
[SPEAKER_00]: of eating for the right reasons, all those moments will disappear.

283
00:21:34,999 --> 00:21:48,826
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that simile given for samādhi, but it's the same principle, like heating up a pan, you're going to have moments of, you know, wet drops of this or this falling onto it, but if the pan is hot, sufficiently hot enough, none of that will linger on the pan.

284
00:21:48,846 --> 00:21:51,627
[SPEAKER_00]: It will evaporate instantly or pretty quickly.

285
00:21:51,647 --> 00:21:53,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the thing.

286
00:21:53,849 --> 00:21:55,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't worry to try to, like, micromanage.

287
00:21:56,763 --> 00:22:00,205
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll take every second dish and not from a person on the left side of the road.

288
00:22:00,225 --> 00:22:07,689
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically like giants, complete, kind of skewed, getting all completely wound up because they didn't know how to approve the lust.

289
00:22:08,530 --> 00:22:15,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what they were doing, they were trying to control the circumstances and just rattle themselves through, like, constantly changing the requirements of the circumstances.

290
00:22:16,715 --> 00:22:18,756
[SPEAKER_00]: But that means you're looking at a completely wrong place.

291
00:22:21,293 --> 00:22:22,413
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll still be eating with lust.

292
00:22:22,853 --> 00:22:25,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're shunning the bait, you're doing it with lust.

293
00:22:25,734 --> 00:22:27,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Lustfully, you don't want to engage with lust.

294
00:22:30,236 --> 00:22:31,256
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't sustain that.

295
00:22:32,216 --> 00:22:33,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sooner or later you have to eat.

296
00:22:34,737 --> 00:22:40,439
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you don't eat, if you die, you died without revealing the true motivation behind the sensuality.

297
00:22:42,060 --> 00:22:43,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you threw away the baby.

298
00:22:49,450 --> 00:22:54,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So, after that, the Buddha disciplines the Bhikkhu further.

299
00:22:54,292 --> 00:22:56,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Come, Bhikkhu, be devoted to wakefulness.

300
00:22:57,153 --> 00:23:03,056
[SPEAKER_01]: During the day, while walking back and forth and sitting, purify your mind of obstructive states.

301
00:23:04,176 --> 00:23:08,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it goes into the, you know, first watch of the night, the second watch of the night, so on.

302
00:23:08,098 --> 00:23:10,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, we did that in the last few talks, didn't we?

303
00:23:10,439 --> 00:23:12,099
[SPEAKER_00]: The overcoming hindrances and so on.

304
00:23:12,539 --> 00:23:18,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Like all... like the liability to lust, that liability to... to... to... aversion.

305
00:23:20,457 --> 00:23:24,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see that that passage is very commonly, very often found in suttas, how you purify your mind.

306
00:23:25,361 --> 00:23:29,203
[SPEAKER_00]: But how many contemporary practices you find teaching you how to purify your mind?

307
00:23:29,224 --> 00:23:34,047
[SPEAKER_00]: They just tell you, focus on your nostrils, do this, be aware of what you're touching, what you're feeling.

308
00:23:34,387 --> 00:23:37,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But how is that purifying your mind of obstructive states?

309
00:23:38,190 --> 00:23:38,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't know.

310
00:23:38,811 --> 00:23:42,834
[SPEAKER_00]: I just do this and I hope nothing arises in my mind that would be obstructive.

311
00:23:43,214 --> 00:23:44,615
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not purifying it.

312
00:23:44,775 --> 00:23:47,257
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to prevent it, trying to get rid of it.

313
00:23:49,133 --> 00:23:58,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the purification, you can't just start with purifying your mind, because you won't even see your mind to begin with, let alone the states that are obstructive for the mind.

314
00:23:59,716 --> 00:24:04,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to be well restrained with discipline, with the eight precepts.

315
00:24:04,058 --> 00:24:05,498
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be celibate.

316
00:24:08,371 --> 00:24:10,292
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see the danger in these faults.

317
00:24:10,832 --> 00:24:20,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to be guarding the sense faculties, not grasping, not welcoming, entertaining the signs and features that will be increasing lust, increasing aversion, and so on.

318
00:24:21,179 --> 00:24:22,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to be moderate in eating.

319
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:26,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another practice that you do on top of these things.

320
00:24:26,402 --> 00:24:28,023
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't just start being moderate in eating.

321
00:24:28,703 --> 00:24:30,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, as I described, it's moderate in eating.

322
00:24:30,684 --> 00:24:33,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about a quantity of bread you ate.

323
00:24:33,886 --> 00:24:36,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about clarity of the motivation behind your eating.

324
00:24:37,229 --> 00:24:39,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And for most people, that's completely invisible.

325
00:24:39,550 --> 00:24:41,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Because most people don't even keep their precepts.

326
00:24:42,852 --> 00:24:43,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people are not celibate.

327
00:24:44,973 --> 00:24:47,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people live with mind wet with sensuality.

328
00:24:47,975 --> 00:24:49,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people don't guard their sense faculties correctly.

329
00:24:50,497 --> 00:24:52,037
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't even see the signs and features.

330
00:24:52,478 --> 00:24:57,080
[SPEAKER_00]: When they hear about guarding the sense faculties, they think, I must not see the eyes... the forms with my eyes.

331
00:24:58,661 --> 00:25:01,143
[SPEAKER_00]: And then just try to beat down the eyes when they do see the forms.

332
00:25:01,923 --> 00:25:04,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not disciplining it, that's just trying to get rid of it.

333
00:25:04,885 --> 00:25:05,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Kill it off.

334
00:25:07,950 --> 00:25:24,201
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do all these things that we went through, then, yeah, you will see the clarity of the mind, there, protected, through the guarding of the senses, through the not... through the... through the sustaining the context in regard to that gateway for lust and non-arisen lust.

335
00:25:24,661 --> 00:25:27,243
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see, no, fine, I'll sustain that, I'll protect that.

336
00:25:27,783 --> 00:25:31,165
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're doubtful, oh, okay, so this is how this obstruction could enter me.

337
00:25:31,846 --> 00:25:35,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that whole talk we did on the liability.

338
00:25:36,397 --> 00:25:37,378
[SPEAKER_00]: What was it?

339
00:25:37,438 --> 00:25:38,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Uprooting versus management.

340
00:25:38,779 --> 00:25:40,040
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the talk, I think.

341
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:44,643
[SPEAKER_00]: The difference between uprooting and just managing.

342
00:25:44,763 --> 00:25:55,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you being watchful, regardless of what you do throughout the day, means mind staying with that which is the priority.

343
00:25:56,371 --> 00:26:00,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about, oh, I have to go and do that, or I have to do this, or I have to stay here.

344
00:26:01,195 --> 00:26:02,176
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is secondary.

345
00:26:03,121 --> 00:26:12,884
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be going there and doing that and staying here, but the primary thing is, I stay here, guarding the gateway towards my mind, so there are no obstructions for it.

346
00:26:13,364 --> 00:26:14,784
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the sole task I have.

347
00:26:15,364 --> 00:26:29,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Task that I have generated and outlined clearly for me now, on account of discipline, guarding the sense faculties, being moderate, careful with motivation in eating, and now I see it clearly, this is the gateway where the obstructions obstruct me,

348
00:26:29,708 --> 00:26:36,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And when they don't obstruct me, if I don't allow them to... Yeah, now you're guarding... Yeah, you're guarding it.

349
00:26:36,511 --> 00:26:39,772
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not fighting, you're not chasing, you're just sitting at a gateway as a guard.

350
00:26:40,632 --> 00:26:41,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And, oh, no, you cannot enter.

351
00:26:43,453 --> 00:26:46,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You are in a position to do so, because you built it up.

352
00:26:47,255 --> 00:26:52,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And the gateway is basically that, like, one entrance into the city that's completely walled around.

353
00:26:52,517 --> 00:26:54,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And the wall around is your discipline.

354
00:26:54,958 --> 00:26:58,559
[SPEAKER_00]: The wall around is your sense restraint and seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

355
00:27:00,979 --> 00:27:03,982
[SPEAKER_00]: The behavior has been purified for beforehand, as the Buddha would say.

356
00:27:04,222 --> 00:27:05,383
[SPEAKER_00]: The wall has been built.

357
00:27:05,423 --> 00:27:05,943
[SPEAKER_00]: No wall.

358
00:27:05,983 --> 00:27:07,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if you have a gate.

359
00:27:07,384 --> 00:27:09,867
[SPEAKER_00]: You can sit at the gate and there is no wall around it.

360
00:27:09,967 --> 00:27:10,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not guarding.

361
00:27:10,947 --> 00:27:12,889
[SPEAKER_00]: You're guarding the gate, maybe.

362
00:27:12,909 --> 00:27:13,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Which nobody is interested in.

363
00:27:13,950 --> 00:27:17,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody wants to go to the city.

364
00:27:17,613 --> 00:27:20,155
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the whole, like, sīla and celibacy is not optional.

365
00:27:21,056 --> 00:27:21,917
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to practice.

366
00:27:23,021 --> 00:27:28,905
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to be a virtuous layperson and stay a layperson, then, yeah, you can keep the five precepts and be generous.

367
00:27:29,346 --> 00:27:31,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what we're talking about, in this talk, at least.

368
00:27:33,268 --> 00:27:45,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, after he's disciplined in being devoted to wakefulness, the Buddha says further, kāmbeku, be possessed of mindfulness and awareness.

369
00:27:45,717 --> 00:27:47,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, it's kind of saying the same thing.

370
00:27:47,319 --> 00:27:50,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's like composing that same wakefulness even further.

371
00:27:53,204 --> 00:28:03,030
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, like, bringing that, that, that context that you have developed, that, that God, God post, that you are clear about, intensifying even further now.

372
00:28:03,611 --> 00:28:08,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through focusing meditation or practice, just being even clearer about it, to the point of

373
00:28:08,634 --> 00:28:11,555
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to sit for half an hour and, like, you know, calm yourself down.

374
00:28:12,056 --> 00:28:26,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're extending your arm, while you're extending your arm, whether you're walking, talking, going to the toilet, eating, even these, like, immediate things that you do that the mind does not necessarily pick on, and doesn't need to pick on in the beginning, even now you're not picking on them.

375
00:28:26,282 --> 00:28:32,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means that even while you're doing it, that context, that guard post, is so clear, you are there.

376
00:28:32,205 --> 00:28:34,706
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not abandoning that one job that you have.

377
00:28:35,326 --> 00:28:37,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Protect the mind from the obstructive states.

378
00:28:38,382 --> 00:28:42,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Where the walking, extending, putting the rope, even these little quick things that you do.

379
00:28:43,743 --> 00:28:45,844
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, at what stage you do that.

380
00:28:46,844 --> 00:28:51,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why I pointed out is because many people would start the practice with that.

381
00:28:51,485 --> 00:28:55,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking, focusing on my hands while I'm touching is doing that.

382
00:28:55,627 --> 00:28:55,767
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

383
00:28:57,247 --> 00:28:58,367
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no focusing there.

384
00:28:58,387 --> 00:29:00,508
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just developing of the context.

385
00:29:01,264 --> 00:29:20,707
[SPEAKER_00]: To the unshakable extent, be celibate, see the danger in the slightest fault, guard your sense faculties, be clear about the motivation in regard to your eating and do not allow yourself to eat out of the unwholesome motivation, watchful, and then you will know what sati-sampajañña is.

386
00:29:22,817 --> 00:29:30,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the simile of a cook, who feeds the king his dishes, and then watches him, and sees, oh, he didn't take that, he took a lot of that.

387
00:29:31,203 --> 00:29:37,528
[SPEAKER_00]: He picks on nimittas of king's preferences, and he pays attention to significance of it.

388
00:29:38,169 --> 00:29:44,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time, he prepares more of that which he picked so many times, and he's skilled in reading nimittas.

389
00:29:44,914 --> 00:29:45,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

390
00:29:45,334 --> 00:29:45,995
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all to it.

391
00:29:47,022 --> 00:29:55,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the other cook doesn't pick on any nimittas, and he just keeps cooking the same thing for king, and he doesn't even eat it, and then the king fires him, sends him to the dungeon.

392
00:29:57,004 --> 00:29:59,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to pick the nimittas off your mind as well.

393
00:29:59,665 --> 00:30:01,005
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your mind inclined to?

394
00:30:01,425 --> 00:30:03,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Pick on that before you gave in to the inclining.

395
00:30:05,306 --> 00:30:05,666
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

396
00:30:07,946 --> 00:30:11,207
[SPEAKER_00]: So, signs and features need to be discerned in the background, peripherally.

397
00:30:11,387 --> 00:30:12,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something you can stare at.

398
00:30:13,867 --> 00:30:16,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there are always signs and features of that which you're staring at.

399
00:30:18,877 --> 00:30:19,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the mind.

400
00:30:20,078 --> 00:30:20,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, seeing the mind.

401
00:30:20,939 --> 00:30:28,888
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see the mind as an object of your senses, but you can discern it peripherally and know it.

402
00:30:28,908 --> 00:30:29,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Cittanimitta.

403
00:30:29,649 --> 00:30:31,851
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole principle behind it.

404
00:30:31,871 --> 00:30:33,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Through this gradual practice.

405
00:30:33,773 --> 00:30:34,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

406
00:30:34,554 --> 00:30:36,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Rounding up, building up the wall, setting up the gate.

407
00:30:37,970 --> 00:30:39,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And... And guiding it.

408
00:30:39,610 --> 00:30:40,731
[SPEAKER_01]: He practices full awareness.

409
00:30:41,551 --> 00:30:49,493
[SPEAKER_01]: The next step is, once he's accomplished in mindfulness and full awareness, the targeter disciplines him further.

410
00:30:49,513 --> 00:30:52,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't be he can resort to a secluded resting place.

411
00:30:52,974 --> 00:30:55,115
[SPEAKER_01]: The forest, the root of a tree, a mountain.

412
00:30:55,155 --> 00:30:56,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Do not dwell in company.

413
00:30:57,175 --> 00:31:00,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But then... and then he goes... and then he resorts to a secluded resting place.

414
00:31:01,016 --> 00:31:01,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Physical seclusion.

415
00:31:01,916 --> 00:31:02,957
[SPEAKER_00]: We did talks on that as well.

416
00:31:03,797 --> 00:31:04,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

417
00:31:06,473 --> 00:31:22,522
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be free from hindrances, which is a prerequisite for entering the jhāna, you need to be dwelling with your mindfulness and recollectiveness thoroughly established.

418
00:31:23,683 --> 00:31:26,785
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to have that established, you need to be well watchful all the time.

419
00:31:27,205 --> 00:31:28,386
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to have that accomplished,

420
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:32,262
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be guarding... no, you need to be moderate in eating, don't you?

421
00:31:32,882 --> 00:31:34,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to be guarding your sense doors.

422
00:31:34,243 --> 00:31:37,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to be celibate and restrained by the virgins of Patimokkha.

423
00:31:37,604 --> 00:31:39,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you want to enter jhānas?

424
00:31:40,346 --> 00:31:43,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Start being celibate here and now.

425
00:31:45,428 --> 00:31:53,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize jhānas, as we often say, are the establishments of mind that come as a result of your purification and the lifestyle.

426
00:31:55,077 --> 00:32:01,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a magical experience that will then take care of your lifestyle for you, which is what people hope.

427
00:32:01,919 --> 00:32:04,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Anti-jhāna, I'm enlightened, now everything is fine.

428
00:32:05,059 --> 00:32:05,199
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

429
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:14,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Jhāna is basically the last symptom, positive symptom, of you healing up everything that needs to be healed.

430
00:32:14,362 --> 00:32:16,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Any wound of lust, aversion, distraction.

431
00:32:18,263 --> 00:32:19,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what every jhāna formula says.

432
00:32:22,584 --> 00:32:23,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Dwell secluded,

433
00:32:25,380 --> 00:32:35,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Removing the grief and concern in regard to the world, abandoning the hindrances, quite secluded from these unwholesome states, the pleasure of seclusion is born.

434
00:32:35,925 --> 00:32:36,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel safe.

435
00:32:37,086 --> 00:32:39,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he enters and abides in jhāna.

436
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:44,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like he enters and abides in jhāna, and that's why he's secluded.

437
00:32:44,422 --> 00:32:44,582
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

438
00:32:44,682 --> 00:32:46,723
[SPEAKER_00]: He secluded himself from unwholesome states.

439
00:32:47,204 --> 00:32:48,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Jhāna is the result of that.

440
00:32:48,784 --> 00:32:49,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what everybody said.

441
00:32:49,685 --> 00:32:51,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a divine abiding.

442
00:32:51,586 --> 00:32:58,170
[SPEAKER_00]: When Tathāgata's mind is in jhāna, whether he walks, eats, goes to the toilet, whatever he does, it's divine.

443
00:33:00,551 --> 00:33:02,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can live in jhāna.

444
00:33:06,821 --> 00:33:09,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that mind, that context, that mind is so purified.

445
00:33:10,125 --> 00:33:17,435
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind's purified, the senses were guarded, the body has been... the senses have been tamed and basically kept within the walls.

446
00:33:18,036 --> 00:33:20,380
[SPEAKER_00]: The guard post has not been abandoned.

447
00:33:21,378 --> 00:33:33,301
[SPEAKER_00]: You have not been delighting in things that would be making lust increase, non-reason lust increase, and non-reason aversion manifest, and so on.

448
00:33:34,161 --> 00:33:39,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, what you have left is just the kind of... the recognition of the nature of things.

449
00:33:40,503 --> 00:33:45,124
[SPEAKER_00]: And you haven't been giving in to that which makes hindrances overwhelm you.

450
00:33:45,865 --> 00:33:48,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're basically not being overwhelmed by the hindrances anymore.

451
00:33:49,266 --> 00:33:50,706
[SPEAKER_00]: You are freed from them.

452
00:33:51,551 --> 00:34:02,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you abandon your celibacy, abandon your guarding of the senses, abandon your moderation eating, as the Buddhists say, you fall off from the jhāna and the suttas.

453
00:34:04,563 --> 00:34:12,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if circumstances change to such a great extent, they just... everything becomes too unbearable, like in case of severe illnesses and so on.

454
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:21,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you haven't abandoned your virtue, but you can't spend your time, you know, guarding the gatepost, because you're overwhelmed by pain, half conscious, half unconscious.

455
00:34:21,763 --> 00:34:24,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Dying, basically, your guts are rotting, and something like that.

456
00:34:24,564 --> 00:34:25,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you might fall away from jhāna.

457
00:34:26,365 --> 00:34:39,730
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if your jhāna has been developed beforehand, even then, you would not necessarily fall away from jhāna, as all the the arahants that could obtain jhānas at will, were able to endure this insane amount of discomfort and pain, because of their jhānas.

458
00:34:41,228 --> 00:34:47,693
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pains, they would be overwhelming a normal mind, but not painful for them, because of the jhānas that they developed.

459
00:34:48,573 --> 00:35:06,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But important thing, you cannot stress that enough, is jhāna is the seventh, whatever, step there, the result of all of this gradual development that, literally, in contemporary Buddhism today, is... almost everything is bypassed, and you get to jhāna first.

460
00:35:07,508 --> 00:35:09,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, how convenient is that?

461
00:35:10,455 --> 00:35:14,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, where the actual work even for the jhāna is, you bypass all of that.

462
00:35:14,996 --> 00:35:18,977
[SPEAKER_00]: And when people say, oh, well, Bhante, didn't the Buddha enter jhāna spontaneously?

463
00:35:18,997 --> 00:35:19,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he didn't.

464
00:35:20,697 --> 00:35:23,338
[SPEAKER_00]: He was under a root of a tree, quite secluded from sensuality.

465
00:35:23,898 --> 00:35:24,658
[SPEAKER_00]: He was probably...

466
00:35:25,299 --> 00:35:28,559
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how old he was, but he was probably not sexually active at the time.

467
00:35:29,240 --> 00:35:31,380
[SPEAKER_00]: He was probably not chasing senses and so on.

468
00:35:31,400 --> 00:35:35,021
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a kid, which means his mind was not turned towards sensuality.

469
00:35:35,541 --> 00:35:39,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, he's abiding, even without a jhāna, was relatively pure.

470
00:35:39,825 --> 00:35:41,847
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't have sensuality and ill-will on his mind.

471
00:35:42,387 --> 00:35:51,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Having been quite secluded from that for a while, that time when he sat under a tree waiting for his father, his mind developed that pleasure on account of seclusion, and that was the jhāna.

472
00:35:52,457 --> 00:35:54,559
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't really know the value of it at the time.

473
00:35:55,760 --> 00:35:59,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, later on, when he thought, what if that were the way for enlightenment?

474
00:36:00,108 --> 00:36:02,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And he reasoned and said, yeah, that probably is.

475
00:36:03,191 --> 00:36:03,751
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me do it.

476
00:36:03,791 --> 00:36:08,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't do it, because, like, I'm overwhelmed by the physical pain of this dying body that I've tortured to the point of death.

477
00:36:09,576 --> 00:36:12,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me eat something, let me regain my health, and then I'll do it.

478
00:36:12,458 --> 00:36:12,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And he did it.

479
00:36:15,441 --> 00:36:17,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still the same process.

480
00:36:18,503 --> 00:36:19,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same steps.

481
00:36:20,245 --> 00:36:22,207
[SPEAKER_00]: The jhāna was the result of withdrawal.

482
00:36:23,007 --> 00:36:26,290
[SPEAKER_00]: If you get your jhāna spontaneously, it can only happen for one reason.

483
00:36:27,678 --> 00:36:32,340
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been thoroughly withdrawn from sensuality on account of your efforts in guarding the sense doors, faculties.

484
00:36:32,820 --> 00:36:35,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are the chances of that happening accidentally?

485
00:36:37,462 --> 00:36:38,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, zero to none.

486
00:36:39,362 --> 00:36:47,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Who can accidentally withdraw themselves from sensuality and see the peril of the danger in sense objects and lust and abandonment?

487
00:36:47,925 --> 00:36:49,006
[SPEAKER_00]: It's virtually impossible.

488
00:36:50,217 --> 00:36:55,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your whole being, your whole existence, is in that direction for pleasure and to avoid pain.

489
00:36:55,259 --> 00:36:58,361
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the definition of human realm, of any realm.

490
00:37:01,662 --> 00:37:03,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Guided by craving in regard to feeling.

491
00:37:03,943 --> 00:37:05,904
[SPEAKER_00]: The fundamental principle behind dukkha.

492
00:37:08,705 --> 00:37:12,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you withdraw yourself from that, you don't have to worry about jhānas.

493
00:37:14,768 --> 00:37:15,408
[SPEAKER_00]: That is jhāna.

494
00:37:15,608 --> 00:37:16,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Withdrawal from that.

495
00:37:18,084 --> 00:37:23,406
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to worry about a technique or a method, which, incidentally, you will not find in the suttas.

496
00:37:26,487 --> 00:37:27,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you will find a method.

497
00:37:27,527 --> 00:37:28,288
[SPEAKER_00]: There it is.

498
00:37:28,488 --> 00:37:38,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Method is celibate eight precepts, guarding the sense doors, moderating eating, danger in the slightest fault, watchful, clear awareness, jhāna.

499
00:37:39,912 --> 00:37:40,892
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need more than one method.

500
00:37:41,132 --> 00:37:42,193
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, I told you that before.

501
00:37:43,650 --> 00:37:48,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Twenty-something years ago, when I started practicing, started reading the suttas, it was Samana Pāla Sutta.

502
00:37:48,572 --> 00:37:49,453
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember I was reading it.

503
00:37:49,793 --> 00:37:52,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was... he has the similar passage there, when the Buddha describes that.

504
00:37:52,554 --> 00:37:53,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a similar kind of training.

505
00:37:53,859 --> 00:37:56,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember reading it at the time, and it was all like, yeah, yeah, this is great.

506
00:37:56,981 --> 00:38:13,711
[SPEAKER_00]: But I still had the views back in the in the early days, like 99, 2000, that... because, you know, everything else you read about Buddhism is about these techniques and experiences, and jhāna 1, jhāna 2, and enlightenment, and you have all these preconceived notions about what enlightenment is.

507
00:38:14,151 --> 00:38:19,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with all that still being in my mind, I was reading sutta, and it was like, kind of, yeah, but come on, tell me exactly.

508
00:38:20,615 --> 00:38:23,256
[SPEAKER_00]: What I do for that jhāna and for that enlightenment.

509
00:38:23,756 --> 00:38:26,577
[SPEAKER_00]: As if everything else I read up to that point was not exact enough.

510
00:38:27,617 --> 00:38:29,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it wasn't exact enough, because I expected more.

511
00:38:30,758 --> 00:38:33,418
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted something more focused, more particular.

512
00:38:34,299 --> 00:38:37,880
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you actually start thinking about it, you say, well, wait.

513
00:38:38,080 --> 00:38:39,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Have I fulfilled the first step?

514
00:38:39,920 --> 00:38:41,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Have I fulfilled the second step?

515
00:38:41,741 --> 00:38:46,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let me fulfill these steps, and then, if I need something for focusing, then I'll ask for focusing.

516
00:38:47,299 --> 00:38:50,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you realize, if you fulfill all these other steps, you will have the jhāna.

517
00:38:51,343 --> 00:38:54,185
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, the Buddha said, like a... like a... what was it?

518
00:38:54,846 --> 00:38:56,127
[SPEAKER_00]: A hand sitting on her eggs.

519
00:38:57,168 --> 00:39:03,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if she doesn't wish for her chicks to piss out safely, they will, because she did the work of sitting and keeping the eggs warm.

520
00:39:04,694 --> 00:39:14,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you fulfill all these steps, perfect your virtue, perfect the indriyājana, the slightest fault, watchfulness, moderate in eating, you will have the jhāna, you will have this passion.

521
00:39:16,060 --> 00:39:19,541
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a matter of, oh, I did everything right, but I still don't have it.

522
00:39:19,681 --> 00:39:19,781
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

523
00:39:20,041 --> 00:39:23,702
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't have it, means you haven't done anything right, or not sufficiently right enough.

524
00:39:25,482 --> 00:39:25,982
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we say.

525
00:39:26,002 --> 00:39:30,523
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible to not have results, if practice has been undertaken correctly.

526
00:39:31,283 --> 00:39:33,164
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you use that to review yourself as well.

527
00:39:33,644 --> 00:39:34,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Are there results to show for?

528
00:39:35,584 --> 00:39:38,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as we used to chant in the temples back in the day,

529
00:39:42,143 --> 00:39:43,483
[SPEAKER_00]: How well am I spending my life?

530
00:39:43,523 --> 00:39:47,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be ashamed when questioned by my companions in holy life?

531
00:39:47,304 --> 00:39:54,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, if I'm dying and my companions come to interrogate me and see if I'm an arahant and how much this person... as they used to do today.

532
00:39:54,126 --> 00:39:56,566
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't do that, but back in the day there was a norm.

533
00:39:56,986 --> 00:39:59,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go before he dies and see what he understood.

534
00:39:59,307 --> 00:40:00,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, how many suttas you got that in?

535
00:40:01,787 --> 00:40:02,768
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you use that.

536
00:40:02,908 --> 00:40:07,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so if I'm questioned on my deathbed, am I an arahant actually?

537
00:40:07,289 --> 00:40:08,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I better become an arahant.

538
00:40:10,729 --> 00:40:17,414
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use that to make yourself strive even more, because if you strive rightly, you would have to be an arahant, or at least an anāgāmī.

539
00:40:18,274 --> 00:40:26,140
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't say at the deathbed, no, no, no, my friends in holy life, I have, guarantee you, 100% fulfill Buddha's instruction, but I'm not even a sotāpanna.

540
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:27,501
[SPEAKER_00]: That is impossible.

541
00:40:28,181 --> 00:40:28,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

542
00:40:33,632 --> 00:40:42,235
[SPEAKER_00]: That is like to say, oh, I've been living in this desert and the sun for 50 years of my life, but these clothes are still wet, miraculously.

543
00:40:42,555 --> 00:40:43,255
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

544
00:40:43,375 --> 00:40:44,436
[SPEAKER_00]: They would have to dry.

545
00:40:48,457 --> 00:40:49,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use that.

546
00:40:49,277 --> 00:40:50,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, so, is there dispassion?

547
00:40:51,778 --> 00:40:56,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there proper seclusion from hindrances, from unreasoned hindrances to arise?

548
00:40:56,540 --> 00:40:58,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see them free from that as well?

549
00:40:59,501 --> 00:41:02,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop clarity in regard to that, you're developing clarity in regard to jhāna.

550
00:41:07,935 --> 00:41:10,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a method.

551
00:41:10,538 --> 00:41:13,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Just not the method that most people would expect, or want.

552
00:41:15,003 --> 00:41:17,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the method you want, but the method you need.

553
00:41:17,866 --> 00:41:18,246
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

554
00:41:18,346 --> 00:41:19,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

555
00:41:19,688 --> 00:41:20,929
[SPEAKER_00]: The method everybody needs.

